{"name":"Hydrangea Floret","hex":"#e6eae0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hydrangea","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several shrubs, of the genus Hydrangea, having large clusters of white, pink or blue flowers."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hydrangea"},{"term":"floret","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small flower, especially one of a cluster in a composite flower."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/floret"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hydrangea","blocks":[{"text":"Hydrangea is a genus of more than 70 species of flowering plants native to Asia and the Americas. Hydrangea is also used as the common name for the genus; some are also often called hortensia. The genus was first described from Virginia in North America, but by far the greatest species diversity is in eastern Asia, notably China, Korea, and Japan. Most are shrubs 1–3 m tall, but some are small trees, and others lianas reaching up to 30 m (100 ft) by climbing up trees. They can be either deciduous or evergreen, though the widely cultivated temperate species are all deciduous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Hydrangea_arborescens_139866012.jpg/330px-Hydrangea_arborescens_139866012.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrangea","wikidata":"Q155997"},{"title":"Glossary of botanical terms","blocks":[{"text":"This glossary of botanical terms is a list of definitions of terms and concepts relevant to botany and plants in general. Terms of plant morphology are included here as well as at the more specific Glossary of plant morphology and Glossary of leaf morphology. For other related terms, see Glossary of phytopathology, Glossary of lichen terms, and List of Latin and Greek words commonly used in systematic names."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glossary_of_botanical_terms","wikidata":"Q2049965"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Hydrangea_Flower_Color_Based_on_Soil_pH.jpg/500px-Hydrangea_Flower_Color_Based_on_Soil_pH.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Hydrangea_Flower_Color_Based_on_Soil_pH.jpg/500px-Hydrangea_Flower_Color_Based_on_Soil_pH.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hydrangea flower color changes based on the pH in soil. As the graph depicts, soil with a pH of 5.5 or lower will produce blue flowers, a pH of 6.5 or higher will produce pink hydrangeas, and soil in between 5.5 and 6.5 will have purple hydrangeas.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHydrangea_Flower_Color_Based_on_Soil_pH.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Hydrangea_paniculata_-_flower_view_01.jpg/500px-Hydrangea_paniculata_-_flower_view_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Hydrangea_paniculata_-_flower_view_01.jpg/500px-Hydrangea_paniculata_-_flower_view_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hydrangea paniculata","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHydrangea_paniculata_-_flower_view_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Hydrangea_knowltoni_as_Hydrangea_bendirei_USNM_P36979_img3.jpg/330px-Hydrangea_knowltoni_as_Hydrangea_bendirei_USNM_P36979_img3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Hydrangea_knowltoni_as_Hydrangea_bendirei_USNM_P36979_img3.jpg/330px-Hydrangea_knowltoni_as_Hydrangea_bendirei_USNM_P36979_img3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hydrangea knowltoni","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/